  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Chilly mornings, hot afternoons with gradual warming trend \n   through the week.\n\n-  Smoke will continue to impact the region until further notice.\n\n-  Ridge of high pressure flattens heading into the weekend.\n   Elevated and localized near-critical fire weather expected. \n\nA ridge of high pressure will continue to build over the western\nU.S. maintaining warm, dry and stable conditions. Strong\nradiational cooling under dry, \"clear\" (but smoky) skies allowed\nKalispell to set a record low of 38 degrees this morning. This\ncooling is creating sharp nocturnal valley inversions which can\naid in trapping smoke near the surface worsening air quality and\nvisibility during the morning hours. Conversely, thermal belts on\nmid-slopes and ridges will keep conditions warm and dry with\nincreasingly poor overnight humidity recoveries (25-35% range), \ncontrasting with excellent recoveries in deep valley bottoms. \n\nAfternoon heating will remain a double-edged sword allowing us to\nmix into cleaner air aloft or any thicker plumes that develop \nfrom regional wildfires and get transported overhead. Current \nhigh-resolution smoke modeling indicates heavier near-surface \nsmoke concentrations peaking Thursday afternoon in the near-term.\n\nTemperatures warm back into the 90s (or warmer) for many lower \nelevations by Friday and Saturday. As the ridge begins flattening\ninto a more zonal flow this weekend, afternoon surface winds will\nincrease. While synoptic winds currently look too marginal for\nFire Weather / Red Flag highlights during any given specific period\nat this time, high temperatures, low humidity values, breezy\nconditions, and low fuel moisture will continue to keep fire \nweather elevated if not near- critical. \n\nA slight uptick in available moisture riding the periphery of the\noverhead ridge may introduce very slight chances for elevated \nconvection from Friday through the weekend particularly across \nsouthwest Montana. Incredibly dry surface humidity could easily \ndrive localized erratic outflow potential into the 30-50 knot \nrange with a risk for new lightning ignitions.\n\nLooking into next week, there is low confidence in a slow uptrend\nof available moisture, but no widespread wetting rains are \nexpected. A major Pacific NW closed low scenario will ultimately \nbe required to induce a rapid downtrend in prevailing fire weather\nand resultant smoky conditions.",
